## Context

Quality grounding review gap G2: the evidence rubric (0–4) classifies
sources by URL domain regex and text patterns (`verify.py:66-96`) but
never checks that the URL exists or contains the claimed content. A
model citing "Decision 1509/QD-BCT" gets score 3 (primary source) if
the format matches, even if the decision number is fabricated. This
undermines the falsifiability claim anchoring the rubric.

## Actions

1. **URL existence check (HTTP HEAD):** for every source URL in
   verification output, issue an HTTP HEAD request. Record: status
   code, final URL after redirects, content-type. Cache aggressively
   (same URL won't change between runs). Flag 404/DNS-fail as
   "dead link" — downgrade evidence score to 1 (no usable source).
2. **Content spot-check:** on a random sample (e.g., 20 plants from
   the best extraction), fetch the page, search for the plant name
   or capacity value in the page text. Record: found/not-found.
   Report the spot-check hit rate as "source content confirmation
   rate." This quantifies the false positive rate in our source
   classification.
3. **Do not block on content verification for all plants.** The
   spot-check is enough to calibrate trust in the rubric. Full
   content verification is a separate, larger effort.

## Test

On the DeepSeek V3.2 decomposition run (our headline result):
- HTTP HEAD succeeds for >80% of cited URLs
- Spot-check on 20 plants: report confirmation rate
- Any dead-link downgrades reflected in updated evidence score
  distribution

## Exit criteria

- URL HEAD check integrated into verification pipeline
- Dead links flagged and evidence scores adjusted
- Spot-check report on 20-plant sample with confirmation rate
- Results mentioned in the article's verification discussion
